hello and welcome to the cheeky science  show where in this video we're going to  talk about the news that came out  yesterday from astrazeneca  that they have now initiated their phase  one clinical trial for monoclonal  antibody combination  for the prevention and treatment of  covert 19. so the aim of this phase one  clinical trial is to assess the safety  tolerability and pharmacokinetics that  is  what the body does to the drug to see if  there's a generation of any anti-drug  antibodies  and the drug that they'll be using is  asd-7442 which is a combination of two  monoclonal antibodies  so what are these mono-colonial  antibodies going to be doing  well the aim of astrazeneca is to  develop a drug  for the prevention and treatment of  cavite 19 so the aim of this drug  is to prevent infection of healthy cells  by the cells called two virus  and whilst it still wasn't completely  understood how this virus is infecting  healthy cells it is pretty well  established  that the way that it's doing it is  through the use of spike proteins  on the surface of sarcophagi and these  spike proteins contain  a receptor binding domain that can  interact with  ace2 receptors on healthy cells to  enable us integration  into healthy human cells so a desirable  goal would be to  block the receptor interaction of the  spike protein with the ac2 receptor  so what do we know about the antibodies  that estrosenica are going to be using  well earlier this year astra seneca  signed an exclusive license to six  candidate antibodies  currently from the vanderbilt university  and they had a nature paper out earlier  this year where they screened lots of  different  potential coronavirus neutralizing  antibodies that  bind to the receptor binding domain on  the spike protein  so astrosenica are going to take two of  these monoclonal antibodies from the six  and progress them into clinical  evaluation as a combination approach  within the next two months  so fast forward two months and here we  are with the two chosen  monoclonal antibodies that astral seneca  further developed  to improve the half-life and according  to their press release the half-life  extended  monoclonal antibodies should afford at  least six months of protection from  covid19  and the other neat thing to notice about  the antibodies is the fact that it's a  combination  it's like a cocktail of two different  antibodies and  the reasoning behind this is based on  the fact that by having two monoclonal  antibodies that bind to distinct parts  of the sars cop2 spike protein  it is hoped that it could improve the  effectiveness of neutralizing the virus  and prevent entry into healthy cells  so this phase one trial is going to  include 48 healthy participants in the  uk  aged from 18 to 55 and so it is hoped  that  based on these results that have an idea  of the tolerability and safety profile  of the drug  and if it's good news then ashley  cenaker will progress it into larger  late stage phase 2 and phase 3 trials to  evaluate its efficacy as a potential  preventative and treatment approach  against covert 19.  so as always i hope you've learned  something and thanks for listening
 

